WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
Privilege management in C starts with a simple observation: some programs can affect more of the system than ordinary application code.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:22.040
They may read or write protected files, open sensitive devices, bind restricted resources, interact with other users, create child processes, update logs, or change operational state that other components depend on.

3
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:31.500
When a C program runs with this kind of authority, mistakes that would normally be local defects can become system-level reliability and security problems.

4
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.060
This does not mean privileged C code has to be mysterious.

5
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.120
It means the design must be explicit.

6
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:50.140
Developers need to know which part of the program runs with elevated authority, why that authority is needed, what data reaches that code, what files or resources it can touch, and how the program behaves when something fails.

7
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.600
Privilege is a boundary for design, review, testing, and operations.

8
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.840
Inputs deserve extra discipline around this boundary.

9
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:11.020
Command-line arguments, configuration values, paths, environment variables, message data, file names, and runtime state should not be trusted just because they arrive before a privileged action.

10
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:19.520
The program should validate shape, range, ownership, location, and intended use before it makes a decision that depends on authority.

11
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.480
Cleanup paths are just as important as happy paths.

12
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:33.500
A privileged program may create files, change permissions, hold descriptors or handles, update locks, start child processes, or write audit events.

13
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.640
If the program exits early or encounters an error, cleanup should still be predictable.

14
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:46.580
Reviewers should ask whether resources are left in a safe state and whether failures are reported without exposing sensitive details.

15
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.980
The main habit for this course is to make privilege visible.

16
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.960
Put privileged assumptions in the design, not only in scattered code comments.

17
00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:02.820
Make the boundary easy to review, keep the powerful parts small, and ensure tests cover both expected behavior and failure behavior.

18
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:09.560
Privileged code becomes safer when teams can explain it, constrain it, and verify it repeatedly.
